Executive Summary

NVIDIA and a collection of chip vendors, server manufacturers, cloud providers, model companies, venture capitalists and enterprise-software firms have published a letter explaining that open-weight artificial intelligence is essential for competition, safety, sovereignty, innovation and American prosperity.

This is an extraordinarily fortunate conclusion for companies that earn money whenever another model is downloaded, adapted, trained, fine-tuned, hosted, benchmarked, served or placed inside an enterprise rack.

The letter’s underlying economic formula is not difficult:

\text{More models}
\rightarrow
\text{more training and inference}
\rightarrow
\text{more compute demand}
\rightarrow
\text{more NVIDIA systems}

The public is invited to admire the openness of the first term while politely ignoring who invoices the final term.

NVIDIA generated $215.9 billion in revenue during fiscal 2026, of which approximately $193.7 billion—nearly 90%—came from its data-centre business. Its gross margin was 71.1%. In the first quarter of fiscal 2027, data-centre revenue reached $75.2 billion out of $81.6 billion total revenue, or just over 92%, while gross margin reached 74.9%. 

Jensen Huang has therefore discovered that open weights are an urgent matter of democratic principle at almost exactly the moment they generate industrial-scale demand for the thing his company sells.

How spiritually convenient.

The letter says organisations should not become locked into a single provider. NVIDIA’s own platform is built around CUDA, NVIDIA drivers, NVIDIA GPUs, NVIDIA libraries, NVIDIA networking, NVIDIA software and paid enterprise licences. NVIDIA describes itself not merely as a chip company but as a full-stack, data-centre-scale AI infrastructure company whose chips, systems, interconnects, software and algorithms are jointly designed. 

So the proposed liberation looks like this:

Model weights: open
Training data: often closed
Training process: often closed
Hardware: proprietary
Driver stack: proprietary
Execution ecosystem: vendor-controlled
Electricity bill: yours
Gross margin: NVIDIA’s

The open-weight case contains legitimate technical arguments. This paper addresses the industrial pantomime in which a dominant infrastructure supplier presents its preferred demand conditions as though they descended from the heavens on a stone tablet marked Competition.

NVIDIA is not the neutral philosopher of this market.

NVIDIA is the till.

