2. Open Weights, Closed Everything That Actually Costs Money

The letter borrows the moral prestige of open-source software and applies it to open model weights.

That analogy is doing unpaid overtime.

Open-source software allowed people to inspect source code, modify it, build it on ordinary machines, distribute changes and reproduce the software’s operation without obtaining permission from a central vendor.

Open weights provide one important artefact: trained model parameters. They do not automatically provide the original training data, complete training code, filtering process, human-feedback pipeline, evaluation history, compute, networking, energy or capital needed to recreate the model.

The user may possess the weights while remaining completely unable to reproduce how they were produced.

That is not meaningless openness. It is simply much narrower than the historical analogy is designed to make it sound.

More importantly, the weights do not run through spiritual intention. They need physical infrastructure.

NVIDIA’s documentation says CUDA applications require a CUDA-capable GPU and a compatible NVIDIA driver. NVIDIA’s annual report describes CUDA as the foundation of a much larger stack incorporating acceleration libraries, APIs, SDKs, models, datasets, networking and paid enterprise software. 

So the glorious open ecosystem works rather like this:

You may inspect the recipe.
The kitchen, ovens, gas supply, utensils,
staffing system and building lease remain proprietary.

NVIDIA’s vision is not open computing.

It is open model proliferation on a closed commercial substrate.

The model may be free.

Running it is the product.

