3. The Anti-Lock-In Sermon From the Architect of CUDA Lock-In

The letter says organisations want confidence that they will not become “locked into a single provider.”

Someone in the drafting meeting apparently survived saying this without laughing.

CUDA is one of the strongest software moats in modern computing. Developers do not merely purchase a GPU. They write against NVIDIA’s programming model, libraries, tooling, compiler behaviour, profiling environment and deployment stack. Over time, applications accumulate dependencies that make changing hardware vendors expensive even when competing silicon exists.

NVIDIA calls CUDA its foundational development platform and says its full-stack model combines GPUs, CPUs, DPUs, switches, networking, libraries, frameworks, APIs and enterprise software. 

That is excellent engineering.

It is also the literal construction of dependency.

The company is now warning that model providers might create the sort of customer lock-in NVIDIA spent years perfecting one layer below them.

Apparently lock-in becomes an ethical emergency when somebody else owns the lock.

The French Competition Authority has explicitly identified risks around technical and financial lock-in in generative AI and has kept NVIDIA’s graphics-card practices under scrutiny. Its investigation into alleged anticompetitive conduct was reported as nearing completion in July 2026; that procedural stage is not a finding that NVIDIA broke the law. 

The irony survives regardless of the investigation’s outcome.

NVIDIA is asking policymakers to protect model-layer competition while its own infrastructure layer has become so strategically central that competition authorities are examining whether its position restricts the same competition it is currently praising.

The translation is simple:

Please prevent anyone above us from becoming as difficult to route around as we are.

