4. The Champion of Competition Who Tried to Buy Arm

NVIDIA’s letter treats competition as the natural guardian of innovation.

NVIDIA previously attempted to acquire Arm for approximately $40 billion.

Arm’s architecture and licensing business are foundational inputs used by numerous chipmakers, including companies competing with NVIDIA. The US Federal Trade Commission sued to block the transaction, alleging that ownership would give NVIDIA both the ability and incentive to disadvantage rivals, reduce innovation, increase prices and reduce choice. NVIDIA and SoftBank abandoned the acquisition amid regulatory opposition. 

This is a remarkable corporate journey:

2020:
Let us buy a foundational architecture used by our competitors.
2021:
Regulators say that could allow us to stifle competition.
2022:
Deal abandoned.
2026:
Competition is sacred and concentrated technological control is dangerous.

NVIDIA did not merely attempt to purchase another graphics-card company.

It attempted to acquire a neutral architectural supplier upon which rival chip designers depended.

Then, four years later, it signed a letter explaining that foundational technology must remain widely accessible so no small group can concentrate power.

The FTC had already explained that to NVIDIA.

The company apparently needed time to convert the complaint into thought leadership.

