5. Open Models Do Not Decentralise the Expensive Part

The letter repeatedly treats wider availability of model weights as though it naturally produces a more distributed AI economy.

That conclusion skips the part containing the buildings.

The US National Telecommunications and Information Administration found that access to weights may broaden research and downstream use, but also concluded that compute and other resource constraints make open weights unlikely, by themselves, to transform competition at the advanced foundation-model layer. 

In plain English:

Possessing the file does not mean possessing the factory.

The frontier remains concentrated because the scarce inputs are not just model parameters. They include accelerator supply, memory, interconnects, energy, data centres, engineering teams and capital.

NVIDIA’s own accounts illustrate that concentration. In fiscal 2026, one direct customer represented 22% of total revenue and another represented 14%. The company also says a limited number of indirect customers each accounted for substantial shares of revenue. 

The supposed decentralised ecosystem is therefore being physically assembled through a small number of enormous purchasers buying from a dominant supplier.

That is not a village workshop.

That is several hyperscalers purchasing industrial quantities of machinery from the same warehouse.

Open weights may expand the number of people allowed to build applications.

They do not automatically distribute ownership of:

* fabrication capacity;
* advanced packaging;
* high-bandwidth memory;
* interconnect technology;
* data-centre power;
* accelerator clusters;
* or the software stack that makes those clusters commercially usable.

The public receives downloadable intelligence.

The physical means of operating it remain concentrated among companies whose quarterly capital expenditure resembles the budget of a country.

Calling that “sovereignty” because the .safetensors file arrived successfully is doing constitutional theory by download bar.

