6. Safety by Adjective

The letter says open weights strengthen safety and cybersecurity.

It then acknowledges that released weights cannot be recalled, modified versions may be difficult to trace, and capable systems can be misused.

The authors nevertheless move directly from “there are competing effects” to “openness may be one of the most important paths to safety.”

No measurement is supplied.

No threshold is supplied.

No comparison is supplied.

No quantified threat model is supplied.

The relevant security expression is closer to:

S_{\text{net}}
=
G_{\text{defence}}
-
G_{\text{offence}}
-
C_{\text{irreversibility}}
-
C_{\text{proliferation}}

where:

* G_{\text{defence}} is the benefit from auditing, red-teaming and distributed repair;
* G_{\text{offence}} is the benefit supplied to attackers;
* C_{\text{irreversibility}} is the cost of being unable to recall released weights;
* C_{\text{proliferation}} is the cost of unrestricted copying and modification.

The letter assumes:

S_{\text{net}} > 0

without estimating any term.

That is not a safety result.

It is a preference wearing a lab coat.

The NTIA’s official review treated open-weight models as a source of both public benefits and public-safety risks. It recommended ongoing monitoring rather than declaring openness inherently safe. 

Open access can help researchers find vulnerabilities.

It can also let attackers remove safeguards without requesting an API key.

Whether defenders or attackers gain more depends on the capability, threat, deployment context, available expertise and time horizon. There is no general theorem saying the benevolent side always receives the larger multiplier.

NVIDIA’s letter converts an unresolved empirical balance into a slogan because slogans fit comfortably beneath company logos.

