7. Distillation Is Learning; Reverse Engineering NVIDIA Is a Licence Violation

The letter tells policymakers not to confuse legitimate distillation with misappropriation.

The distinction is real. Training one model using the outputs of another is not automatically theft.

But NVIDIA’s philosophical generosity becomes rather more selective when the technology being examined belongs to NVIDIA.

Its software licences restrict reverse engineering, decompilation, disassembly and the creation or distribution of unauthorised derivative works. Some NVIDIA terms specifically prohibit translating generated artefacts for use on non-NVIDIA hardware. 

Again, model distillation and software reverse engineering are not legally identical. The revealing feature is the direction of the moral instinct.

When extracting capability from a model expands the AI ecosystem:

Humanity has a long tradition of learning, building upon and improving technology.

When extracting or translating NVIDIA’s proprietary software threatens its platform position:

Please consult Section 2.15 of the licence agreement.

The operational philosophy is:

Your behaviour:
A valuable source of training signal.
Our implementation:
Protected intellectual property.

Or more concisely:

Open weights for your model. EULA for my moat.

NVIDIA’s preferred line is not “information should be free.”

It is:

Information should flow freely whenever that flow produces more accelerator demand and stop immediately whenever it allows the accelerator to be replaced.

