8. Sovereignty, Delivered in a Box You Cannot Manufacture

The letter repeatedly uses the word sovereignty.

Organisations supposedly become sovereign because they can download a model, adapt it and deploy it on their own infrastructure.

But a country dependent on imported accelerators, foreign fabrication, foreign high-bandwidth memory, foreign networking and a proprietary software platform does not possess full technological sovereignty.

It possesses local custody of a dependency.

NVIDIA itself says its supply chain is concentrated in Asia and identifies export controls, power availability, water availability, capital and data-centre construction as material constraints on its business. 

Downloaded weights do not fabricate silicon.

They do not produce advanced packaging.

They do not create memory supply.

They do not build substations.

They do not remove export controls.

They do not replace CUDA.

So the proposed national sovereignty looks like this:

Nation:
We possess the model.
NVIDIA:
Excellent. Here is the approved hardware stack,
driver stack, networking stack, software stack,
support contract and invoice required to operate it.

That is greater autonomy than exclusive dependence on a closed API.

It is not independence.

It is choosing which floor of the dependency tower to rent.

