9. The Letter Accidentally Admits That the GPU-Only Future Is Bad Engineering

Buried beneath the policy language is one genuinely excellent principle:

Use the right model for the right job and reserve frontier-scale capability for genuine frontier problems.

Correct.

Now finish the sentence:

Use the right processor for the right operation and reserve premium accelerators for work that benefits from them.

NVIDIA’s own filings say GPUs excel at massively parallel workloads such as neural-network training and inference, while its broader systems also require CPUs, DPUs, networking and specialised software. 

A deployed AI service does far more than multiply tensors.

It routes jobs, validates inputs, sorts records, maintains state, performs exact arithmetic, manages checkpoints, updates logs, applies policy, filters tables, handles sparse structures, produces evidence, executes deterministic transforms and recovers interrupted work.

Using frontier accelerator capacity for every surrounding task is not sophistication.

It is bad factory layout.

It is using a touring car to tow pallets around the yard because the touring car happens to be expensive and already running.

A competent heterogeneous architecture would look more like:

GPU / model accelerator
    Dense tensor work
    Training
    Attention
    Probabilistic inference
CPU
    General applications
    Operating system
    Flexible orchestration
Purpose-built deterministic fabric / ASIC / FPGA / sidecar
    State
    Exact bounded jobs
    Sparse and irregular operations
    Checkpointing
    Recovery
    Evidence
    Reproducible execution

The important engineering question is not:

How can every workload be made to consume another GPU?

It is:

How much premium accelerator capacity can be removed by sending each operation to the hardware designed for it?

NVIDIA’s letter embraces the “right model for the right job” because small and specialised models still need to run somewhere.

It becomes strangely silent at “right hardware for the right operation,” because the best answer may sometimes be not an NVIDIA GPU.

That is where the open ecosystem reaches the edge of the map.

Beyond it, the cartographer has written:

Here be alternative architectures.

