10. Economically Sustainable, Provided Energy Has No Opportunity Cost

The letter says billions of everyday AI tasks must become economically sustainable.

It offers open weights as a major part of the solution.

Yet it contains no serious accounting of electricity, grid capacity, cooling, water, embodied manufacturing cost or the opportunity cost of assigning premium hardware to routine work.

The International Energy Agency estimates that data-centre electricity consumption rose to approximately 485 terawatt-hours in 2025 and could reach around 950 terawatt-hours by 2030. It expects electricity use by AI-focused data centres to triple over that period. 

So when NVIDIA proposes diffusing AI into every factory, farm, hospital, classroom and small business, the engineering question is not merely whether the weights can be downloaded.

It is:

\text{Useful output per joule}

Not:

\text{Number of models announced per keynote}

Open weights can enable smaller and more efficient models. They can also multiply deployments, duplicated infrastructure and total inference demand.

Whether the net effect saves energy or consumes more is measurable.

The letter does not measure it.

It simply declares the expansion economically sustainable while the company selling the accelerators warns investors that shortages of power, water, land and data-centre capacity could constrain future revenue. 

The public document says:

Diffuse intelligence everywhere.

The investor document says:

Please build enough power stations for us to continue doing that.

Electricity is treated as a background texture because electricity does not have a logo to place beneath the signature line.

